SingularityFlow — Complete Manual Walkthrough
Clone a repository → create authority → describe the capability → create a workspace → run the first story

Verified against main 82fd8ef (Sept 16, 2026). Windows cmd.exe syntax where it differs is shown in comments. Everything runs on your machine; no services.

0. Prerequisites (once per machine)
bash
node --version          # 20 or newer
git --version
git config --global user.name  "Ashok Raj"
git config --global user.email "ashok@example.com"

# Install the CLI (from the singularityflow checkout, via Artifactory in corp):
./install.sh            # or: npm install -g <artifactory package>
singularity-flow --version
1. Clone the repository (plain Git — nothing special)
bash
git clone https://git.example/payments.git C:\work\payments   # Windows path; ~/work/payments elsewhere
cd C:\work\payments

If the remote already carried governed state, plain singularity-flow onboard . would attach to it and you would skip step 2. For a new, never-governed repository, inspect-repository finds nothing (as you saw: Remote branch sflow/config not found) and step 2 creates the authority.

2. Create the authority in this clone (no remote contact, works offline)
bash
singularity-flow onboard . --bootstrap --policy unmanaged-local-v1 --authority-local
. is the local checkout path — there is no URL here by design.
Creates the sflow/config branch inside this clone, pins it, writes a receipt.
Verify:
bash
singularity-flow doctor --json
git branch --list "sflow/*"

To let a second person see the governed state later (optional now):

bash
git push origin sflow/config
3. Describe the capability (what this repository is, what it owns)
bash
# The capability and what it owns:
singularity-flow capability add payments-core --owns "src/**" --name "Payments Core" --team payments-eng

# Protect what needs an owner's signature (example):
singularity-flow capability protect src/auth/** --capability payments-core --approver security --reason "credential handling"

# See the result:
singularity-flow capability tree --json
singularity-flow capability show payments-core --verbose

add/protect author the checkout directly — right for a standalone pilot. (When an organisation lead exists, the governed route is capability map payments-core --lead <lead-url> --repository <this-repo-url> ..., which pushes a review branch against the lead's sflow/config; adopt later with capability adopt-managed --preview → --confirm sha256:<plan>.)

4. Create a workspace (the set of repositories you work across)

For a single-repository pilot with no capability map, use the no-map form:

bash
singularity-flow workspace create --local --id pay-ws --name "Payments work" ^
  --lead https://git.example/payments.git ^
  --repository payments=https://git.example/payments.git ^
  --base C:\work --confirm pay-ws
# (^ is cmd.exe line continuation; use \ on macOS/Linux)
Registers the workspace under C:\work\pay-ws and clones what is missing (--no-clone to reuse the clone from step 1; --dry-run to preview).
If a clone is interrupted on the corporate network: workspace bootstrap status, then workspace bootstrap resume <ID> --confirm pay-ws.
Health: singularity-flow workspace doctor --network

When an organisation map exists instead: workspace create --local --id pay-ws --organisation <lead-url> --capability payments-core --base C:\work --confirm pay-ws

5. Start the first story
bash
cd C:\work\pay-ws\payments      # or your step-1 clone if you used --no-clone

# Write the story (title + acceptance criteria), or point at Jira instead:
# (cmd.exe: create story.yml in an editor; the heredoc below is bash)
cat > story.yml <<'YML'
title: Add request caching to the pricing service
summary: Reduce repeated upstream calls
acceptanceCriteria:
  - Quotes for identical requests within 60s are served from cache
  - Cache is bypassed when a request carries no-cache
YML

singularity-flow start PAY-101 --from-branch main --story-file story.yml --work-type quick-fix
# Jira instead of a file:  singularity-flow start PAY-101 --jira --from-branch main --work-type quick-fix

This creates the story branch and isolated working copy, and pins the rules, work-type and capability resolution for the whole story — later config edits stop the story rather than silently changing it.

6. Work the story to publication
bash
singularity-flow status --json        # what is owed, what is met
singularity-flow next                 # the one legal next action (it will guide every step below)

singularity-flow prepare implement    # brief and templates for the phase
# ... edit code in this working copy (or let Copilot draft via /sflow-work-interval) ...

singularity-flow precheck             # what publish would say right now
singularity-flow phase publish implement
singularity-flow approve implement    # reviewer signs the exact version
                                      # (self-approval is refused; use a second Git identity)

singularity-flow explain <refusal-or-receipt-id>   # why anything happened, from records

The one-minute proof at the end: edit any published file, run singularity-flow status — the approval shows expired, because a signature binds to exact bytes. That behavior is the product.

Quick reference — the whole flow
Step	Command
Clone	git clone <url> <dir>
Authority (new repo)	singularity-flow onboard . --bootstrap --policy unmanaged-local-v1 --authority-local
Attach (governed repo)	singularity-flow onboard .
Capability	capability add <id> --owns "src/**" · capability protect <path> --approver <group>
Workspace	workspace create --local --id <ws> --lead <url> --repository <id>=<url> --base <dir> --confirm <ws>
Story	start <ID> --from-branch main --story-file story.yml --work-type <type>
Loop	next → prepare → edit → precheck → phase publish → approve
Anytime	status --json · doctor --json · explain <id>
